: 1611}}, "post_flair_text": "", "archive_id": ""}, {"is_locked": false, "tweet_id": "", "sticky_comment": 0, "is_video_mp4": false, "type": "text", "uuid": "1ATVMHOJKz", "is_twitter": false, "is_admin": false, "score": 21, "suggested_sort": 0, "post_flair_class": "", "id": 8849391, "raw_content": "Following a temporary diplomatic agreement and memorandum of understanding signed on June 17, 2026, the United States briefly lifted its naval blockade in the Strait of Hormuz. Tehran used this window to rapidly clear out its pent-up crude supplies\n\nThe Volume: Iran deployed a \"shadow fleet\" of about 20 tankers to rush out nearly 70 million barrels of oil between mid-June and mid-July\n\nThe Pace: In the second half of June alone, Iran exported 50 million barrels\u2014an amount equivalent to a full pre-war month of exports to China\n\nThe Evasion Route: To obscure the origin of the oil and bypass lingering sanctions, the tankers utilized ship-to-ship transfers using large hoses in international waters off the eastern coast of Malaysia. From there, the disguised crude was delivered to China's independent, private \"teapot\" refineries, which frequently purchase discounted oil from sanctioned nations\n\nThough the U.S. collapsed the truce and reinstated its blockade \"Operation Economic Outcast\" shortly thereafter, oil analysts report that the $6 billion surge successfully allowed Iran to build a massive financial buffer. Experts from United Against Nuclear Iran (UANI) note that if the blockade had remained continuously active, Iran's economy would have faced a severe supply pinch by late summer; instead, the brief window provided the regime with a significant economic lifeline\n\nIran shipped $6 billion worth of oil to China using shadow fleet during brief US blockade pause: Report\n\nMost of the oil was shipped to China using a network of tankers that helped Tehran bypass sanctions. ", "author": "OrangeManNotBad", "created": 1790051955791, "profile_picture": "", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "grenades_and_ham", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "", "removal_source": "", "link": "", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [], "blurhash": "", "full_resolution": []}, "title": "Iran successfully moved between $5 billion and $6 billion worth of crude oil toward China by exploiting a brief, roughly one-month pause in U.S. maritime restrictions", "last_comment_created": 1790066315940, "content": "<p>Following a temporary diplomatic agreement and memorandum of understanding signed on June 17, 2026, the United States briefly lifted its naval blockade in the Strait of Hormuz. Tehran used this window to rapidly clear out its pent-up crude supplies</p>\n<p>The Volume: Iran deployed a &quot;shadow fleet&quot; of about 20 tankers to rush out nearly 70 million barrels of oil between mid-June and mid-July</p>\n<p>The Pace: In the second half of June alone, Iran exported 50 million barrels\u2014an amount equivalent to a full pre-war month of exports to China</p>\n<p>The Evasion Route: To obscure the origin of the oil and bypass lingering sanctions, the tankers utilized ship-to-ship transfers using large hoses in international waters off the eastern coast of Malaysia. From there, the disguised crude was delivered to China's independent, private &quot;teapot&quot; refineries, which frequently purchase discounted oil from sanctioned nations</p>\n<p>Though the U.S. collapsed the truce and reinstated its blockade &quot;Operation Economic Outcast&quot; shortly thereafter, oil analysts report that the $6 billion surge successfully allowed Iran to build a massive financial buffer. Experts from United Against Nuclear Iran (UANI) note that if the blockade had remained continuously active, Iran's economy would have faced a severe supply pinch by late summer; instead, the brief window provided the regime with a significant economic lifeline</p>\n<p>Iran shipped $6 billion worth of oil to China using shadow fleet during brief US blockade pause: Report</p>\n<p>Most of the oil was shipped to China using a network of tankers that helped Tehran bypass sanctions.</p>\n", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_stickied": false, "is_nsfw": false, "is_deleted": false, "is_image": false, "comments": 7, "score_up": 21, "is_edited": false, "is_moderator": false, "awards": 0, "vote_state": 2, "link_metadata": {"status": 200}, "post_flair_text": "", "archive_id": ""}, {"is_locked": false, "tweet_id": "", "sticky_comment": 0, "is_video_mp4": false, "type": "link", "uuid": "1ATVMHOJ4W", "is_twitter": false, "is_admin": false, "score": 24, "suggested_sort": 0, "post_flair_class": "pepegif", "id": 8849354, "raw_content": "", "author": "CleanUpPhillyTrump24", "created": 1790049294349, "profile_picture": "", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "MAGA_is_kosher1", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "media.scored.co", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "https://media.scored.co/preview/1ATVMHOJ4W.png", "removal_source": "", "link": "https://media.scored.co/post/KZisSUZy6eX7.jpeg", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [{"reference": "/scale/JHvpmzo9kbUY4tpu.jpeg", "width": 500, "content-type": "jpeg", "height": 551}], "blurhash": "U3G8+OM000cu004$R.~N009s~qIC0O~Q%M0f", "full_resolution": [551, 500]}, "title": "GN Frens. Rest up for strong week of winning.", "last_comment_created": 1790049722922, "content": "", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_stickied": false, "is_nsfw": false, "is_deleted": false, "is_image": true, "comments": 1, "score_up": 24, "is_edited": false, "is_moderator": false, "awards": 0, "vote_state": 2, "link_metadata": {"status": 200}, "post_flair_text": "\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0PEPE\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u